1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,231
Here's something that should be simple.

2
00:00:03,431 --> 00:00:08,794
You've got USDC on an exchange. You want
it in a savings app on Arc.

3
00:00:09,344 --> 00:00:13,241
So you send it… and the app has no idea
it's yours.

4
00:00:14,891 --> 00:00:18,647
Exchanges and wallets can only send to an
address.

5
00:00:18,797 --> 00:00:21,281
But apps keep money in smart contracts,

6
00:00:21,331 --> 00:00:25,180
and those only accept it through their own
deposit function.

7
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:27,854
That's how they know who to credit.

8
00:00:28,204 --> 00:00:33,193
Sending is easy. Getting it into the right
contract is the hard part.

9
00:00:34,143 --> 00:00:36,221
Doorstep closes that gap.

10
00:00:36,471 --> 00:00:40,980
It turns an ordinary USDC transfer into a
proper deposit,

11
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:43,700
credited to the right person.

12
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:45,629
Here's how.

13
00:00:45,779 --> 00:00:50,361
First, the app sets the instructions: the
contract, who gets credited,

14
00:00:50,411 --> 00:00:52,150
and where refunds go.

15
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,011
Those instructions produce an address.
Change anything,

16
00:00:56,061 --> 00:00:58,122
and you get a different one.

17
00:00:58,672 --> 00:01:01,212
The payer just sends their USDC there,

18
00:01:01,262 --> 00:01:04,543
from a wallet or straight from an
exchange.

19
00:01:05,093 --> 00:01:10,441
Then a delivery bot calls the contract's
deposit function for them. If it can't,

20
00:01:10,491 --> 00:01:13,607
the money goes back to the refund wallet.

21
00:01:14,557 --> 00:01:16,859
And it's live on Arc mainnet.

22
00:01:17,109 --> 00:01:20,097
In the demo, you get your own savings
address,

23
00:01:20,147 --> 00:01:23,535
with a QR code and the instructions behind
it.

24
00:01:24,085 --> 00:01:27,785
Send a few cents, and watch it arrive in
your savings.

25
00:01:28,335 --> 00:01:32,511
Every payment gets a receipt, and
Doorstep's cut is zero.

26
00:01:32,861 --> 00:01:38,342
Behind it: an SDK on npm, a hosted API
with signed webhooks,

27
00:01:38,392 --> 00:01:42,153
and a bot that runs around the clock.

28
00:01:43,003 --> 00:01:47,971
Arc is what makes this practical, because
gas is paid in USDC.

29
00:01:48,221 --> 00:01:53,843
The bot spends USDC to deliver, and it's
repaid in USDC from the payment,

30
00:01:53,893 --> 00:01:55,098
with a cap.

31
00:01:55,348 --> 00:01:59,280
No second token. No price oracle. No
swaps.

32
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:04,043
Apart from that capped fee, money only
goes two places:

33
00:02:04,093 --> 00:02:09,219
into the contract in the instructions, or
back to the refund wallet.

34
00:02:09,569 --> 00:02:14,165
Nobody can redirect a payment, and there's
no admin key at all.

35
00:02:14,615 --> 00:02:17,963
It isn't audited yet, so keep the amounts
small.

36
00:02:18,713 --> 00:02:22,271
And because it's just an address, any app
can use it.

37
00:02:22,421 --> 00:02:25,581
Savings. Escrow. Invoices. Payroll.

38
00:02:25,831 --> 00:02:31,697
Even AI agents: our MCP server lets them
create, check and deliver payments,

39
00:02:31,747 --> 00:02:34,821
but never send money anywhere else.

40
00:02:35,971 --> 00:02:38,759
Your users' money is already out there.

41
00:02:39,009 --> 00:02:41,482
Doorstep gives it a way in.
